  <!-- Developer Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ) Accordion -->
  <section style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 1.4rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem; color: var(--text-primary);">Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>

    <div class="faq-container">
      <!-- Item 1: Auto-Expanded by Default -->
      <details class="faq-item" open>
        <summary>
          <span>What makes Playground API different from JSONPlaceholder?</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          While standard mock APIs like JSONPlaceholder return fake responses when you send a <code>POST</code>, <code>PUT</code>, or <code>DELETE</code> request without saving your changes, Playground API saves your mutations into a <strong>per-session sandbox overlay</strong> tied to your HTTP cookie or <code>X-Playground-Identity</code> header token. When you perform subsequent <code>GET</code> requests, your created, updated, or deleted items persist specifically for you without altering global seed data for other developers.
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- Item 2 -->
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>
          <span>Do I need an API key or account registration?</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          No. Playground API is 100% free and open-source. No API keys, user sign-ups, or authentication tokens are required to start prototyping. Simply make standard HTTP requests using <code>fetch</code>, <code>axios</code>, <code>cURL</code>, or Postman.
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- Item 3 -->
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>
          <span>How do I use Playground API in cross-origin apps, Cypress, or Postman?</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          For cross-origin frontend applications (React, Vue, Next.js) or Cypress/Postman tests where third-party cookies might be blocked, pass the <code>X-Playground-Identity: &lt;signed-token&gt;</code> header in your HTTP requests. You can copy your active signed token instantly from the top navigation bar or sidebar drawer.
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- Item 4 -->
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>
          <span>How long do session sandbox mutations persist?</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          Your session overlay data persists via your identity token and is maintained in the database for up to <strong>10 days of inactivity</strong>. An automated daily cleanup job purges inactive identities automatically. You can also purge your sandbox instantly at any time using the <code>DELETE /session/reset</code> endpoint or the "Reset Sandbox" button in the docs.
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- Item 5 -->
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>
          <span>How does Network Delay and HTTP Error Simulation work?</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          You can test frontend loading states and resilience by introducing artificial latency (0–20,000ms) with the <code>X-Simulate-Delay: 1500</code> header or <code>?_delay=1500</code> query parameter. To test error boundaries, pass <code>X-Simulate-Status: 500</code> or <code>?_status=404</code> to force custom status error responses.
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- Item 6 -->
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>
          <span>What are the rate limits and record creation quotas?</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          General read requests are limited to 60 requests per minute per IP hash. Mutation requests (<code>POST</code>, <code>PUT</code>, <code>DELETE</code>) allow up to 15 requests per minute with a quota cap of <strong>30 self-created sandbox records per resource</strong> per session to prevent abuse.
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- Item 7 -->
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>
          <span>How do I export API collections for Postman, Bruno, or Insomnia?</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          You can export OpenAPI 3.0 Specs, Postman v2.1 Collections, Bruno Collections, and Insomnia files with one click from the sidebar or resource documentation pages. Use the resource filter dropdown in the sidebar to download schemas for specific collections (<code>/users</code>, <code>/posts</code>, <code>/comments</code>, <code>/todos</code>) or all resources at once.
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- Item 8 -->
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>
          <span>Is full-text search and dynamic sorting supported?</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          Yes! Append <code>?q=&lt;term&gt;</code> to perform case-insensitive full-text search across all record fields (e.g., <code>GET /posts?q=javascript</code>). For sorting, use <code>?_sort=&lt;field&gt;&amp;_order=asc|desc</code> (e.g., <code>GET /posts?_sort=title&amp;_order=desc</code>). Search and sorting operate dynamically across merged global database records and your local session overlay.
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- Item 9: GraphQL Sandbox Gateway -->
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>
          <span>Does Playground API support GraphQL queries and mutations?</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          Yes! Playground API features a full-featured <strong>GraphQL Sandbox Gateway</strong> at <code>POST /graphql</code> powered by the exact same stateful per-session sandbox engine. You can query baseline data, fetch nested relational fields (e.g., <code>user { posts { comments } }</code>), execute mutations (<code>createPost</code>, <code>deleteUser</code>), and test Apollo/Relay clients. An interactive <strong>GraphiQL IDE</strong> is also available at <code>GET /graphql</code> or via the <a href="/docs/graphql">GraphQL Docs</a> page.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>
